-- title:
--   Invertibility of the ideal generated by a global non-zero-divisor on an affine scheme
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be an affine scheme and let $r$ be a global section of $X$, i.e. an element of $\Gamma(X,\top)$, which is a non-zero-divisor in that ring. Consider the quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data `Scheme.IdealSheafData.ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {r})` attached to the principal ideal $(r)\subseteq\Gamma(X,\top)$. The assertion is that this ideal sheaf data satisfies the project's predicate `IsInvertible`, which unfolds to: for every point $x$ of $X$ there exist an affine open $U$ of $X$, a section $f\in\Gamma(X,U)$ with $x$ lying in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\,f$, and an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f)$ which is a non-zero-divisor of that ring, such that the ideal assigned by the ideal sheaf data to the affine open $X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f$ equals $\mathrm{span}\{g\}$. Thus the ideal of $r$ is, locally on $X$ and in the sense of this predicate, generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the statement that on an affine scheme the closed subscheme cut out by a global non-zero-divisor is an effective Cartier divisor, in the local form used by the project's `IsInvertible` predicate. It serves as the base case for invertibility assertions elsewhere, being cited in the comparison of the inverse module with the tensor unit and in the treatment of ideal sheaves arising from resolutions of crossing quotients.
